[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: csw1
diagnosis_text:
There is no physical link failure on the CSW1<->R1 segment. Both ends show a healthy carrier:
- csw1 eth0: ethtool reports “Link detected: yes,” speed 10G, RUNNING, and IP 10.0.0.34/30.
- r1 eth0: ethtool reports “Link detected: yes,” speed 10G, RUNNING, and IP 10.0.0.33/30.

The affected component is OSPF on CSW1, specifically interface eth0. A recent configuration change set CSW1’s eth0 as a passive OSPF interface, which suppresses OSPF hellos and prevents adjacency formation on that segment. Evidence:
- CSW1 FRR config: “interface eth0 … ip ospf passive”.
- CSW1 “show ip ospf interface” on eth0: “No Hellos (Passive interface)” and Neighbor Count 0.
- R1 “show ip ospf interface” on eth0: DR with Neighbor Count 0 (no peer seen).
- R1 maintains OSPF only over eth1 with CSW2 (routes via 10.0.0.38), matching the symptom that Internet traffic uses the CSW2 path.

Localization: CSW1, interface eth0 (CD_WAN_LINK_1) OSPF process; R1 eth0 link is up but lacks OSPF neighbor due to CSW1 suppression.

Root cause: CSW1 eth0 was inadvertently configured as an OSPF passive interface during the routine change.

Corrective fix: No recabling is required. On CSW1, remove the passive setting on eth0 (vtysh: conf t; interface eth0; no ip ospf passive) or under router ospf use “no passive-interface eth0.” This will restore OSPF adjacency with R1 on eth0 and reestablish the dual-path behavior. === END ===